label acceptance
[signal arg="bg:training_hall"]
He said yes three days after Amir made the offer,
standing in the same training hall where he'd first heard the man tell sixty first-years that rank meant nothing.
Amir: Good. We start now. Not tomorrow. Now.
Lan (Default): I'm still healing.
Amir: You're healing. You're not dead. Those are different states, and only one of them excuses you from work.
Amir: Show me your circulation. Full output, nothing held back, nothing rationed for an audience that isn't here.
For the first time in his entire second life, Lan felt what it meant to stop apologizing for his own strength.
Amir watched with the flat patience of a man cataloguing every leak, every wasted thread,
and said nothing encouraging for the better part of an hour, which Lan had begun to understand was its own kind of compliment.
Amir: You're a mess. A talented mess. We fix the mess first.
Amir: Shir comes after, once your foundation stops leaking like a cracked jug. Tomorrow. Same time. Don't be late.
set {stat_control} += 2
label quiet
[signal arg="bg:cafeteria_morning"]
The weeks that followed had a shape Lan hadn't felt since before the accident that ended his first life.
Ordinary ones, stacked one on top of another, nothing trying to kill him in any of them.
Amir before the sun was properly up. The montante in the east wing with Ilia.
Classes, meals, the particular chaos of people who'd decided, without discussing it, that they belonged to each other.
Enriko: I've decided that Doc secretly runs a gambling ring out of the infirmary. I have no proof.
Enriko: I simply refuse to believe a man that calm is doing nothing interesting with his free time.
Liesel (Flat): That's insane.
Enriko: Is it, though?
Liesel (Flat): I have asked him what he does on weekends. He reads.
Enriko: A cover story, if I've ever heard one.
Veldero: You've built an entire criminal empire out of a man liking books.
Enriko: I have instincts, Veldero. They rarely fail me.
Veldero: They fail you constantly.
Florentine (Smile): You're all children. I don't know why I like any of you.
Enriko: Because we're delightful.
Liesel (Smug): Debatable.
Lan (Sus): (I've never had this. Not the trophies, not the tournaments, not even the quiet mountain house.)
Lan (Sus): (People who stack themselves around a breakfast table because they want to, not because a plot requires it.)
Lan (Sus): (I didn't examine that too closely. I was afraid, a little, of what I'd find.)
[signal arg="freetime:ch15"]
label kliam_said
[signal arg="bg:records_office"]
Leonora (Default): He won't give up a single name. Not his suppliers, not his buyers, not a location.
Leonora (Default): Three weeks of questioning, and the interrogators have gotten one thing out of him that wasn't a joke at their expense.
Leonora (Default): A warning. I wrote it down.
Leonora (Default): "You're asking the wrong question.
Leonora (Default): You should be asking what happens to people who go looking for the man who actually runs this. I've met him twice.
Leonora (Default): Once was one time too many."
Lan (Sus): (A set of initials on a folded note and a hooded shape in a stable that never had a face. And I don't know what either of them means.)
Lan (Default): If a man like Kliam is scared of someone, I'd like to know who before they decide to be interested in either of us.
Leonora (Default): So would I.
label ledger
He caught her once, late, in the same records office, a fresh page covered in her small, precise handwriting.
Names, dates, arrows connecting one column to another.
Leonora (Default): Building a case. Quietly. Properly.
Leonora (Default): I want something that holds up in front of my father, not just something that satisfies me.
Leonora (Default): Edric is careful and he has resources I don't, and I'm not willing to move before I'm certain,
Leonora (Default): because the first accusation I make against him is the only one I'll get to make cleanly.
Lan (Default): Let me know if there's anything I can do.
Leonora (Smile): You've already done more than you know, Schwert. Go to bed. You have training in six hours.
set {aff_leonora} += 1
label announcement
[signal arg="bg:notice_board"]
It came at the end of the month, posted on every department board at once.
MID-YEAR EVALUATIONS. FULL RANK RECALCULATION.
Every first-year, every department, thrown back into the fire at once: practical combat, theoretical mastery,
shir control, everything that had built the current list, subject to being rebuilt from nothing over a single brutal week.
Enriko: Well. This should be interesting for you specifically.
Lan (Default): Don't.
Enriko: I'm simply noting that a certain someone has spent the last month training under the academy's head sword instructor in secret,
Enriko: and a certain evaluation is about to ask that someone to stop pretending otherwise. {rank} is about to have a very bad week, historically speaking.
Raiz: Schwert.
Raiz stood at the edge of the crowd, arms folded, reading the notice with no expression at all.
Raiz: The list gets rebuilt. I intend to still be first. Where do you intend to be?
- "Where you are. I'll come for first."
	set {aff_raiz} += 2
	Lan (Smug): Wherever you are. I'll come for first.
	Raiz: Good. A rival with a number is useless. A rival with a target isn't.
- "Alive. That's the plan."
	set {aff_raiz} += 0
	Lan (Default): Alive. That's the whole plan.
	Raiz: Modest. Try not to let it become the ceiling.
Lan (Sus): (The number {rank} beside my name, for what I suspect, with a strange mix of relief and vertigo, is about to be the last time.)
label luna
[signal arg="interlude:on"]
[signal arg="bg:closed_room"]
Far from the academy, in a part of the capital that didn't appear on any map a student would be handed,
Luna sat alone in a room with the shutters closed.
A wormhole no larger than a coin opened silently in the air above her desk, and an envelope dropped through it,
sealed in black wax with a symbol she'd learned, a long time ago, never to ignore.
The handwriting inside was elegant, mocking, and utterly certain of itself.
Luna. Hope the disguise is holding up. Try not to strangle anyone this semester, it complicates my paperwork.
The boy is the one.
I've watched enough of this particular story loop to know a load-bearing anomaly when I see it,
and Lan Schwert is currently propping up rather more of this timeline than he understands.
Attach yourself to him. Guard him.
Assist him in whatever way the situation calls for, official or otherwise,
and do try to be subtle about it, subtlety being somewhat outside your usual repertoire.
Do this properly and we both get what we want eventually. Him, whatever it is people like him actually want.
Me, five minutes alone with a particular Evil God and several centuries of accumulated grievance.
Don't disappoint me. It's been a very long time since anything has, and I've grown bored of the streak. N.I.
Luna read it twice, folded it with careful hands,
and sat for a long moment in the closed-shutter dark before she allowed herself something that might, on anyone else's face, have passed for a smile.
Luna (Serious): Finally.
For the first time since arriving at Tausend Sterne, she began to actually plan.
set {luna_letter_seen} = 1
[signal arg="interlude:off"]
[signal arg="season_end:s1"]
[signal arg="chapter_end:ch15"]
